{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3206","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3206","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3206\",\"parent_revision\":\"ea909409da13d71f591282b168591a7e656b1a37947c41376eb75af1e30aea74\",\"root_revision\":\"ea909409da13d71f591282b168591a7e656b1a37947c41376eb75af1e30aea74\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nGoal: end the second surface of implementation duplication between `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` and `plugins/development-harness/dh_core/operations.py`.\\n\\nAn inventory of every MCP tool in `server.py` (recorded in full as \u00a71c of `.tmp/scratch/reports/operations-delegation-design.md`) found 43 tools split three ways: 28 already delegate to `dh_core.operations`, 2 are correctly server-scoped with no operations counterpart, and 13 reimplement logic that also exists in `operations.py`. Four of those 13 are the artifact quartet, tracked separately. The remaining nine are the dispatch family, and they are this item.\\n\\nImpact \u2014 this duplication is not theoretical. It has already produced two observed defects on the artifact side, both found in PR #3203:\\n\\n1. `artifact_id` was added to MCP `artifact_read` in PR #3174 but not to its sibling `artifact_get`, even though the two other surfaces (`dh_core.operations` and the CLI) both carried it. One surface gained a parameter and its twin silently did not.\\n2. The two implementations' error strings drifted apart. `dh_core.operations.artifact_get` reported an unmatched id as \\\"No artifacts of type 'X' found\\\" while entries of that type existed \u2014 the wrong correction, on the surface the CLI shows a caller.\\n\\nThe dispatch family carries the same structural exposure and no equivalent audit has been run against it. Any parameter, validation rule, or error message added to one dispatch surface can diverge from the other without any test failing.\\n\\nUser story: as an agent calling a dispatch MCP tool, I get the same behaviour, parameters, and error text as the CLI calling the equivalent `dh_core.operations` function, because there is one implementation rather than two that must be kept in step by hand.\\n\\nPayoff: removes the mechanism by which the artifact defects occurred, rather than detecting each recurrence after the fact. PR #3203 added a parametrised MCP/operations parity test across all four artifact tools; that test detects divergence but cannot prevent it, and no equivalent parity test exists for the dispatch family at all.\\n\\nExisting observations that add context:\\n\\n- `server.py` already imports `from dh_core import operations` at line 25, so the wiring exists and is in active use by the 28 delegating tools. The nine are not blocked on any missing seam.\\n- The design brief established why the duplicated bodies exist at all, and it is not simple drift: `server.py` handlers let exceptions propagate so FastMCP converts them to `isError=true` responses (see AGENTS.md, \\\"SAM/backlog MCP error contract\\\"), while `operations.py` functions are sync and carry different validation. Any delegation must preserve that contract rather than assume the two bodies are interchangeable.\\n- `operations.py` was created by commit 548ce55ba (\\\"feat(dh): unified backend extraction\\\"). The 13 reimplementing tools were left behind by that extraction; they are not a later regression.\\n\\nUser-provided context: the design brief's recommended approach for the artifact quartet was pre-validation in the tool body followed by `await asyncio.to_thread(operations.<fn>, ...)`, with a before/after probe matrix run against the live MCP server to prove behavioural identity. Whether that same shape fits the dispatch family is a grooming question, not an established requirement \u2014 \u00a71c of the brief has the per-tool inventory to assess it against.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Design brief .tmp/scratch/reports/operations-delegation-design.md \u00a71c, produced while investigating the artifact_get/artifact_read parameter drift found in PR #3203\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ebdf01b688f879a621d289233a933242feecf5219cee483366bf0958822fc2b8\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQTRscg\"}"}